===Etymology 2===
Late Old English (from ca. AD 1000) ''laȝu'', plural ''laȝa'', from an early Old Norse ''*lagu'' (whence Old Icelandic ''lǫg'').
The Old Icelandic word means "something laid down or fixed", both in the literal sense of "layer, stratum" and in the figurative "agreed share", "fixed price", "partnership", etc. The plural had the collective sense of "[body of] law".

The Common Germanic would have been ''*lagom'', from an Indo-Europan root ''*logh-'' (the root of [[lie]], [[lay]]; the Latin ''[[lego]]'' "gather", whence ''[[lex]]'' "law" is not generally considered a cognate).

The natively Old English word replaced by the Old Norse loan was ''[[æ|ǽ]]''.

